cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Detected or Proven: Signed Witness Boundaries Against Quiet-Class Compromise of LLM Agents (consent-substrate reference implementation)"
message: >-
  If you use this software or its evaluation methodology, please cite it
  using this metadata.
type: software
authors:
  - name: Consent Substrate Working Group
version: v1.1.1
date: "2026-10-30"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23797526
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/consent-substrate-wg1/consent-substrate"
abstract: >-
  The most dangerous intrusions into LLM-agent deployments are not the ones
  that look dangerous. This stack replaces look-based detection with two
  signed mechanisms sharing one Ed25519 trust root: a rules-of-engagement
  gate (is this action authorized?) and a canary substrate (did this actor
  touch a witness?), joined into one evidence forest in which an attack is
  either detected or proven, with no third option.
keywords:
  - LLM agent security
  - prompt injection
  - authorization
  - canary tokens
  - hash chains
